<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.innovatiopr</groupId>
    <artifactId>payments-parent</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>payments-domain</artifactId>
  <name>payments-domain</name>
  <description>Aggregates, value objects, domain errors and domain events. Depends on the JDK.</description>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    THIS POM IS THE ARCHITECTURE.

    Look at what is missing: no Spring, no Hibernate, no JPA, no Jackson, no
    servlet API, no Bean Validation, no database driver. None of it is on this
    module's compile classpath, so `import org.springframework.…` inside an
    aggregate does not merely violate a convention - it does not compile.

    That is a stronger guarantee than any test can give, and it is the whole
    reason the layers are separate Maven modules instead of separate packages.

    The single dependency is spring-modulith-api, and it earns its place:
      * `provided` scope, so it is absent from the runtime classpath here;
      * its only mandatory transitive dependency is jspecify (annotations only) -
        spring-context, spring-tx and spring-boot-autoconfigure are all declared
        <optional> upstream, so none of them arrive;
      * it is needed solely for the @ApplicationModule annotations on the
        bounded-context package-info files, which declare the module graph that
        ModularityTest verifies.

    Verify the claim rather than trusting it:
        ./mvnw -pl payments-domain dependency:tree
    ============================================================================
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-modulith-api</artifactId>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
